Here's a clear and concise C# program that implements the insertion sort algorithm:

```
using System;

namespace InsertionSort {
    class Program {
        static void InsertionSort(int[] arr) {
            int n = arr.Length;

            for (int I = 1; I < n; i++) {
                int key = arr[i];
                int j = I - 1;

                while (j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {
                    arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
                    j--;
                }

                arr[j + 1] = key;
            }
        }

        static void Main(string[] args) {
            int[] arr = { 7, 3, 9, 2, 10, 1 };

            InsertionSort(arr);

            Console.WriteLine("Sorted array:");
            foreach (int I in arr) {
                Console.Write(i + " ");
            }
            Console.WriteLine();
        }
    }
}
```

The `InsertionSort` method takes an integer array `arr` as input and sorts it using the insertion sort algorithm. The array `arr` is modified by the method and no value is returned. The time complexity of the algorithm is O(n^2) and the space complexity is O(1).

The `Main` method is a sample usage of the `InsertionSort` method. It creates an integer array `arr`, sorts it using the `InsertionSort` method, and prints the sorted array to the console.

